  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation to $y(x)$ at the point $a=1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =y(a)+{y}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =y(1)+{y}^{\u2032}(1)(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the initial condition, we know that $y(1)=2.$ We also know that ${y}^{\u2032}(x)=x{y}^{2},$ so\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(1)=1\\cdot {2}^{2}=4.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our values for $y(1)$ and ${y}^{\u2032}(1)$ into our expression for $L(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =2+4(x-1) \\\\  & =2+4x-4 \\\\  & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
